  "query_preview": "..\nCurrent radar shows weak showers around Kitt Peak\nwith cloud cover elsewhere across the CWA. Light shower \nactivity will be possible for the remainder of the night, likely\ndissipating by early morning. Aloft the monsoon ridge is \ncentered over the AZ/NM border and will slowly drift eastward \ninto New Mexico later today. PWAT values range from 1.5\" near \nAjo to 1.0\" along the AZ/NM border. This afternoon and evening \nexpect another round of scattered convection from central Pima \nCounty eastward. Storms likely won't be quite as strong as \nThursday looking at HREF probabilities. But still can't rule out\nand isolated severe wind threat. Surface dewpoints will be in \nthe 50s and model soundings show inverted V profiles with DCAPE \naround 1500 J/kg. Initial convection will likely be over the \nSky Islands, then move into the lower deserts progressing \nwestward along outflows. High temperatures will be 6-8 degrees \nabove normal with the Extreme Heat Warning still in effect \nthrough Saturday.\n\nThis weekend the ridge becomes centered over the southern plains\nand a weak trough moves into southern California keeping \nArizona under southerly mid-level flow. Models show a slight \nincrease in moisture with PWAT values increasing by about 0.20\".\nSurface dewpoints also increase to the low to mid 60s. This \nwill lead to an increase in monsoon activity over the weekend \nand into Monday. Expect daily scattered thunderstorms during the\nafternoon and evening with the main threats being strong to\nsevere outflows and locally heavy rainfall leading to flash\nflooding. It's also possible for some overnight activity\nSaturday and Sunday nights thanks to synoptic scale forcing as\nthe weak trough moves through the region. Depending on how\nactive the nights are, this could lead to one or more down days.\n\nAfter Monday troughing stays in place to the west and over the \nPacific Northwest keeping Arizona under southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow. Moisture values stay slightly above normal, \nbut storm chances decrease as a more transitional monsoon \npattern sets up. Still expect daily afternoon and evening \nthunderstorm chances but coverage will likely be reduced with \nthe best chances along the international border. Temperatures \nalso decrease to around normal Sunday into the middle of next \nweek.",
